          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, but this should still be checked by a clinician.** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in its fixed list. That is not a clearance. Breathlessness on exertion without chest pain can still be a sign of a heart or lung problem, and it can be missed because it doesn't feel like the classic warning sign. The Coronary artery disease guidance notes that breathlessness on exertion without chest discomfort is a recognized presentation, and it is more common in older people and people with diabetes. [Source: Coronary artery disease, General clinical reference]\n\n**It should not be put down to age without an assessment.** Getting puffed on a hill can be linked to age, but it is also a symptom that a clinician would want to check. The physical activity guidance says breathlessness that is out of proportion to effort should be assessed before starting vigorous activity. [Source: Physical activity for high blood pressure, Lifestyle guidance]\n\n**Seek emergency help now if you have any of these:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly.\n- Being unable to speak in full sentences.\n- Blue or gray lips, tongue, face or fingertips.\n- Chest pain, pressure or tightness, including pain spreading to the arm, neck, jaw or back, or with sweating or nausea.\n- Fainting, confusion or unusual drowsiness.\n\n[Source: Severe difficulty breathing, Emergency guidance; Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack, Emergency guidance]\n\n**Book a prompt appointment with a doctor if:** the breathlessness is getting worse, you need to stop more often on hills or stairs, you notice palpitations (a racing or irregular heartbeat) when active, or you have known heart or lung disease or high blood pressure.\n\n**General areas a clinician may want to rule out:** these are not diagnoses. The heart and the lungs are the main systems involved. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is one lung condition they might ask about, and coronary artery disease is one heart condition. Infections can also cause breathlessness. [Sources: Coronary artery disease; COPD; Influenza, General clinical references]\n\nI can't tell you what is causing this for you. Please speak to a doctor or nurse about it, and seek urgent help if any of the warning signs above appear.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
